{#
  The page shell every page uses: head tags, fonts, styles, script, the top banner, the small row
  under it (hidden on the home page), the slot for the page's own content, and the footer.
  When a visitor follows a link, only the content slot is swapped ("data-taxi-view"); the banner,
  the small row and the footer stay in place. "data-page" names the kind of page, which decides
  how the change is animated (see assets/js/transitions.js).
  On the home page, two short scripts make the headline slide in when the page opens: one in the
  head and one after the content. See assets/js/intro.js.
  The first script in the head adds the visitor counter (Rybbit), and only on the live site. It
  also keeps jumps within a page (to a "#section") from being counted as page views; for that the
  content slot says which page it holds ("data-path").
-#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ site.language }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>{% if title %}{{ title }} | {% endif %}{{ site.name }}</title>
  <meta name="description" content="{{ description or site.description }}">
  {#- The preview card that WhatsApp, LinkedIn, X and similar apps draw when a link to this page
      is shared. They read these tags: the page's own title and description, its full address,
      and the full address of the share image (src/assets/img/share.png, made by "npm run share").
      The "og:" tags are the Open Graph tags most apps read; the "twitter:" ones are X's own.
      The page for a missing address gives no address, as it has none of its own. #}
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.name }}">
  <meta property="og:title" content="{{ title or site.name }}">
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{ description or site.description }}">
  {%- if page.url != "/404.html" %}
  <meta property="og:url" content="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
  {%- endif %}
  <meta property="og:image" content="{{ shareImage.url }}">
  <meta property="og:image:type" content="image/png">
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="{{ shareImage.width }}">
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="{{ shareImage.height }}">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ site.shareImageAlt }}">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ title or site.name }}">
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ description or site.description }}">
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ shareImage.url }}">
  <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="{{ site.shareImageAlt }}">
  {%- if xHandle %}
  <meta name="twitter:creator" content="{{ xHandle }}">
  {%- endif %}
  <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ site.themeColor }}">
  {#- The site's icon, a compass (scripts/icons.mjs makes the files). The .ico is for older
      browsers and comes first; browsers that can use the drawing take that one, which switches
      between ink and paper with the color of the browser's tabs. The last two are for phones. #}
  <link rel="icon" href="{% siteIcon "ico" %}" sizes="32x32">
  <link rel="icon" href="{% siteIcon "svg" %}" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="{% siteIcon "touch" %}">
  <link rel="manifest" href="{% siteIcon "manifest" %}">
  {%- include "partials/fonts.njk" %}
  {%- if site.analytics %}
  <script>
    /* Visitor counting (Rybbit), on the live site only. This adds Rybbit's own tag to the head, exactly as its install page gives it:
       <script src="{{ site.analytics.script }}" data-site-id="{{ site.analytics.siteId }}" defer>
       It is added by this script instead of being written straight into the page because a written tag cannot tell where it is: it would also count visits to the preview on your own computer and the pages npm run check opens. Here it is added only when the page is being viewed at {{ liveHost }}. The head stays in place through page changes, so the tag is added once per visit and never again. */
    /* Jumps within a page are not page views. Clicking a line under "Contents" on a project's page adds "#section" to the address, and the browser announces that with two events, "popstate" and "hashchange" (it does the same when the back and forward buttons move between such jumps). Rybbit counts a view whenever it hears either. The two listeners below are added before Rybbit's and before anything else on the page, so they hear the events first, and they stop an event from going any further when it is only a jump within the page now showing. The address still changes and the back button still works: only the announcement is cut short.
       - "hashchange" is only ever sent for a jump within a page, so it is always stopped.
       - "popstate" is also sent when the back or forward button leads to another page, which must go through (the page-change script and Rybbit both need it). It is stopped only when the address still belongs to the page being shown ("data-path" on the content slot). One more case goes through: while a move to another page is still under way (the content that was showing when it began, "leaving", is still there), so that the page-change script can deal with a quick back-then-forward itself. */
    var leaving = null;
    addEventListener("hashchange", function (event) { event.stopImmediatePropagation(); }, true);
    addEventListener("popstate", function (event) {
      var shown = document.querySelector("[data-taxi-view]");
      if (!shown) return;
      if (shown.getAttribute("data-path") === location.pathname && shown !== leaving) event.stopImmediatePropagation();
      else leaving = shown;
    }, true);

    if (location.hostname === "{{ liveHost }}") {
      var counter = document.createElement("script");
      counter.src = "{{ site.analytics.script }}";
      counter.setAttribute("data-site-id", "{{ site.analytics.siteId }}");
      counter.defer = true;
      document.head.appendChild(counter);
    }
  </script>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if pageKind == "home" or entrance %}
  <script>
    /* The headline slides in when the page opens (the home page, and any page that says "entrance: true" at the top of its file). Marking the page here, before anything is drawn, lets the styles hide the headline until its letters are ready to move. Not for visitors who asked for less motion, not while npm run check is photographing (it sets steadyMotion), and never for longer than three seconds. */
    if (!window.steadyMotion && !matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) {
      document.documentElement.classList.add("intro");
      setTimeout(function () { document.documentElement.classList.remove("intro"); }, 3000);
    }
  </script>
  {%- endif %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{% asset "/assets/css/main.css" %}">
  <script src="{% asset "/assets/js/main.js" %}" defer></script>
</head>
<body>
  {%- include "partials/banner.njk" %}
  {%- include "partials/page-row.njk" %}
  <div data-taxi>
    <main data-taxi-view data-page="{{ pageKind or 'plain' }}" data-path="{{ page.url }}">
      {{ content | safe }}
    </main>
  </div>
  {%- if pageKind == "home" or entrance %}
  <script>{% introScript %}</script>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- include "partials/footer.njk" %}
</body>
</html>
